WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.160
- There's a kind of feeling

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.160
for the ambition of this
interdisciplinary work

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.630
that often is very happy.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.267
It's like, look, there's
all these disciplines

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.130
and these different people doing research.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.890
If they talk to each
other and they interact,

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.090
things can go really great,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.740
you get this cross-pollination,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.750
you can get these insights.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.490
And that's true, I'm not denying that,

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.323
but...

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.660
cane codes and rabbits were
introduced into Australia

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.132
to pretty disastrous results too.

00:00:39.132 --> 00:00:40.770
Sometimes,

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.710
you take something works
perfectly well where it is,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.880
and you try and use it
somewhere else inconsiderately

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.250
and you get bad results.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.050
So I'm gonna talk about a case

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.810
where I think people have
been getting bad results.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.760
Hopefully, they'll stop doing it.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.649
But also, as a general cautionary tale.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:00.960
Now, sitting at the center,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.960
this talk is gonna be some
claims about Bell's theorem

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.970
and the violation of Bell's theorem.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:06.990
In an ideal world,

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.870
I could explain that for
people who don't understand,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.733
if you don't know anything about it.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.300
Tim Nguyen, actually, I did an
interview with him recently.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.590
It's two and a half hours long,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.720
and it'll go through
in all detail you want

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.980
what that's all about.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.340
I'll just cut it to the
chase at a certain point

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.010
and tell you what Bell proved

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.420
and what the experiments proved.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.250
And if you don't believe me,
it'll take longer to get to.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.580
Okay, so...

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.250
Philosophers and scientists

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.610
often try to represent causal structure

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.340
in various idealized and simplified ways.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.710
And I want to talk about, sorry.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.060
Yeah, I gotta stay here,
this is gonna kill me.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.410
In various idealized ways,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.170
and their gross simplifications

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.700
and less gross simplifications.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.080
I just want to talk about two ways

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.690
that philosophers have used to represent,

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:01.260
and scientists have used to
represent, causal structure.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.030
This first one are examples

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.250
of what are called neuron diagrams.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.400
There's a nice book by
Ned Hall and Laurie Paul

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.120
on causation that use a
lot of these diagrams.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.980
And again, it's extremely idealized,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.740
very simple, like binary kind of system.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.350
The circles represent events

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.500
and the edges, the neurons,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.483
are either excitory, or
excitatory, or inhibitory.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.900
The ones with the arrows excite.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.690
So the idea is, and time goes up on this,

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.640
so you have events at the bottom,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.620
and then that can excite a later event,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.170
and the little circles are inhibitory

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.940
so that'll prevent that from happening.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.340
And then you can kind of read it upwards.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.250
And then given such a diagram,

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.820
given the inputs at the
bottom, you sort of figure out,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.980
given that's the structure,
what's gonna happen.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.170
Now, these two diagrams are for an example

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:59.130
that Ned has used along a long time

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.460
that has these two kids, Billy and Susie,

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.000
who are just vandals and they're
throwing rocks at windows.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.780
And they're both aiming
at the same window.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.930
And this is a example in what's
called in the literature,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:13.923
late prevention.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.400
We can imagine they
throw at the same time,

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.140
but Billy's a little faster.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.453
His rock is just ahead of Susie's.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.020
His rock hits the window, shatters it.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.843
Her rock sails right
through the broken window.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.427
Even if he hadn't thrown, the
problem is, you might say,

00:03:33.427 --> 00:03:34.890
"Well, how do I know that Billy..."

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.677
You say, "Billy, you
broke that window, right?"

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.550
But Billy can say, "Look,
if I hadn't thrown,

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.680
this window still would've broken, right?

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.450
Because her rock would've broken it."

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.700
So it's kind of causal over determination,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.903
late prevention case.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.410
So don't blame me, right,

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.490
if you use a very simple
counterfactual analysis.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.540
And so these two structures
are obviously different.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.760
They give you different causal paths.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.930
The one on the left is
if Billy throws early

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.220
and the one on the right

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.163
is if Susie is slightly early, okay?

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.970
And you'll notice up there,
the only difference there...

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.560
Symmetric, except at the very end

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.140
where I've put that
little inhibitory thing,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.390
that is, if Billy's goes through,

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.190
then not only does he break the window,

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.130
but he inhibits or prevents Susie's

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.900
from breaking the window, right,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.250
'cause it's already broken.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.710
And it's the other way around if she...

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.690
Now, the red circles

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.540
are supposed to be things
you might be able to control.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.280
So the big question is,

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.810
if you have this very idealized picture

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.580
of the underlying causal structure,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.460
okay, how can I figure out what
the underlying structure is?

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.800
'Cause there's certain things I can do

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.603
or I can observe, right?

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.990
Some things I can control,
some things I can observe,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.670
some things are latent or hidden,

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:54.990
and this is what I'm trying to get at.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.040
I can observe the result,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.650
I can see whether the window broke or not.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.070
And I'm imagining you can
have these two interventions.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:06.150
You can, or you may or may
not stay Billy's hand, right?

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.660
Prevent him from throwing or
prevent her from throwing.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.440
And then if you excite those things,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.090
you'll see what happens, right?

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.100
What's clear is that if
you stay Billy's hand

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.100
in this diagram, if you
don't, he's gonna break...

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.720
The chain will be...

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.630
This causes, Billy's throw
causes the window to break

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.330
and prevents hers from breaking it.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.780
If you stay his hand, then
those neurons won't fire,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.880
hers will go straight through,
the window will break,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.350
she'll be responsible.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:38.040
And then you just reverse
the roles of Billy and Susie

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.650
if she's throwing a little faster.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.503
Okay, so I hope everybody
sees how to read these things.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.250
Now, you can ask, all right,

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.040
let's suppose we have
constraints on what we can see.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.430
We can see actually
whether Billy, or Susie,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.380
or both throw their rocks.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.420
We can see whether the window breaks,

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.610
but we can't see the fine details

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.773
of the intervening process, okay?

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.270
And then what you...

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.010
That one back, sorry.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.000
Then what you notice is that

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.250
from what we can observe,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.280
we can't figure out which
of those two diagrams,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.930
if either is correct,

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:19.955
because they give rise

00:06:19.955 --> 00:06:22.500
to the same surface appearance, right?

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.330
What we'll find is that if we
stay neither of their hands,

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.170
the window breaks.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.090
If we stay one or the
other of their hands,

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:30.923
the window breaks.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:32.250
And if we stay both of their hands,

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:33.510
the window doesn't break.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.760
But that's in common
between those two graphs.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.400
So you have two different
underlying causal structures

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.060
that are giving rise to the
same empirical observations,

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.970
and therefore you now have
an epistemic problem, right?

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.170
You say, "I can't really be sure

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.780
what the underlying
causal structure is here."

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:49.613
Good.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.530
Now, let's suppose we
can do something else.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.540
Now I want to suggest, this
isn't in the literature,

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:55.980
I just wanna suggest something else,

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.720
which isn't usually talked about.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.970
Suppose there's a different action.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.660
Suppose I can't stay
Bobby's or Susie's hand,

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.880
but I can delay it, right,

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.593
just slow him down a little bit.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:09.900
What effect can that have?

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.070
Well, suppose if I don't delay it,

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:18.330
they throw, Billy's a little
faster, his rock will hit.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:19.800
So we're on this side.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.510
But if I delay his hand enough,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.640
so now Susie's rock is a little faster,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.410
what I will have done is change you

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.320
from this causal structure
to that causal structure.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.066
So that's a kind of meta
causation, if you will, right?

00:07:31.066 --> 00:07:35.340
It's an effect on the
very causal structure.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.460
But because I'm switching
between two causal structures

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.160
that are on the surface identical,

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.290
I also can't tell that.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.540
I can't tell whether
my staying Billy's hand

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.670
had any effect at all, right?

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.130
Because in fact, on the causal structure,

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:53.130
'cause in fact, if he
had thrown first it did,

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:54.360
but if he'd thrown second,

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.010
it didn't matter that
I delayed him, right?

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.540
It's gonna be the same either way.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:59.910
So I can't figure out the
counterfactual dependency,

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.410
I can't see the counterfactual
dependencies there.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:04.283
Good.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:07.380
And so this is the lasso.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.520
In this case,

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.120
whether or not the delay
has any effect at all

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.733
cannot be gleaned from
the observable data.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:21.330
If you give me the actual
underlying structure, of course,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.010
I know if I know all the details,

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.490
I can tell you whether the
delay made a difference or not,

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.727
but I can't tell it observationally.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.233
So what's the moral,

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:34.650
the reason that the delay

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:36.510
will have no observable consequences

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:39.120
is 'cause the two different
causal structures themselves

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.940
have all the same observable consequences.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:44.610
Then, hence the effect of the delay.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:46.230
Indeed, whether it had any effect at all

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.630
cannot be determined by observation.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:51.960
And it's also obvious that
someone who can only see

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.120
whether or not the window breaks

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.510
cannot possibly tell
whether a delay was put in

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.580
on either Bobby or Susie, right?

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.200
So again, you have this other thing,

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.150
which is because whether
or not I put in this delay

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.480
has no observable consequences downstream.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:12.540
I can't use it for signaling, right?

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:15.540
If I'm trying to, to
send somebody a signal,

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.720
and that person, all they
can see is the window, right?

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.530
And all I can do is either
delay Bobby or not delay Bobby,

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.263
I can't use my ability to do that.

00:09:25.263 --> 00:09:26.970
I may be doing something,

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.400
I may be actually switching
the causal structure,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.890
but I can't signal with it, okay?

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.190
This is a very simple
case where that happens.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.110
So that's...

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:38.220
We would say in this setup,

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.890
I would call this a
no-signaling result, right?

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.420
Given the causal structure,

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.037
this intervention can't
be used to send signals.

00:09:45.037 --> 00:09:48.330
It may actually have a
causal influence, right?

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.080
It may change the causal situation.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.530
Now, the other thing,

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.020
I mean these neuron diagrams
are very, very simple.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.150
You use them for
evaluating counterfactuals

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.790
and you use them for probing
certain counterfactual analyses

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:00.870
of causation.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.030
A more serious thing that scientists use

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.653
are, of course, causal graphs.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.310
They're much more subtle
because they're not just binary.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.580
The neuron diagrams, either
the neuron fires or it doesn't.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.480
It's just a very simple kind of thing.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.790
But now, we again have these nodes,

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:19.980
we're gonna put down a directed graph.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.210
We're gonna have nodes.

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.310
The nodes are connected to other nodes.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:28.310
And one node firing can
have either an excitatory

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.630
or inhibitory effect

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.653
on whether the probability
of another node firing.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:38.030
And...

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:44.160
in the neuron diagrams,
there's no probabilities.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.540
It's all ones or zeros.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.880
But, in a linear causal model,

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.610
and again, you're now
gonna be using these...

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.490
Sorry, you're gonna be using these

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:56.460
similar-looking directed graphs,

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.370
but they're different because
in a linear causal model,

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:02.820
the edges indicate a
kind of causal influence,

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.310
and that can have
different strengths, right?

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.250
So you also have to put
these numbers on the edges

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:11.940
that tell you how
strongly will the effect,

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.750
will the likelihood of the
effect go up or go down

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.140
depending upon how strongly
this thing is firing?

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.810
And the simplest case
mathematically, of course,

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.640
is for that to be a linear relation,

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:27.570
that, again, is obviously
a vast oversimplification.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.880
In the real world, all
these things are nonlinear,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.710
but for small variations,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.610
you can always approximate
something pretty well linearly.

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:39.817
So we have this.

00:11:39.817 --> 00:11:41.970
A linear causal graph is, again,

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:45.090
a self-consciously a great idealization,

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.590
but you can calculate the stuff.

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:47.940
So it's nice.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:52.140
Now, you have the same problem,

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.440
and this has been the
subject of a lot of research

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:56.940
and really active research.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.670
I was around when Clark
Glymour was doing this stuff,

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:02.160
starting this stuff back in the 1980s,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.223
about the same time Judea Pearl
was looking at these things.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:10.200
You set up these graphs, and
then you can notice, again,

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.600
there are certain things
you can control or observe

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.700
and other latent nodes you can't.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:15.690
And more importantly,

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.730
the actual underlying structure is latent.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:19.590
You can't see the underlying structure.

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.140
So what you're trying to
do is see what you can see

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:23.520
and then infer what you can infer

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.100
about the underlying structure
from the actual data.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:28.050
But what you immediately see

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.300
is just as in the Billy-Susie case,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.850
there are gonna be lots of these graphs

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:36.150
where they'll give rise to
the same observable behavior,

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.640
even though they have a
different underlying structure.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.560
And the other thing you see immediately

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.990
if you do the combinatorics

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.720
is that as soon as you
get a lot of these nodes,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:47.160
the combinatorics kills you.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.350
I mean, the number of
different directed graphs,

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:54.350
you can write down, goes very high.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:56.910
I mean, what Glymour was
working on at the time

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.320
was search, right?

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.620
How do I cut down the search
space of graphs I look at?

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.060
Because I can't look at all of them,

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:04.413
it's just not practical.

00:13:07.887 --> 00:13:09.720
The usual constraint, in this case,

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:12.330
you're using directed acyclic graphs

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.910
because you think of
these edges as causation

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.280
and you think causes have
to proceed their effects.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.740
So the causes can't go
around in circles, right?

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:23.612
So that automatically cuts you
down to the acyclic graphs.

00:13:23.612 --> 00:13:25.980
But still they're way too many of them,

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.483
easy to calculate how many.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:32.010
So here, you have to assign these weights.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.200
Now, another funny thing happens here,

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.633
which is because you're
assigning these weights,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:39.900
what you expect to happen

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.483
is gonna depend on the
exact weights you put down.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:48.530
And another thing is from a certain node,

00:13:49.287 --> 00:13:52.200
you can have multiple of these edges

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:55.650
that represent different
causal influences, right?

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:58.530
Or you can have an edge that
goes from here to this one

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.990
and another edge that goes
from this, to this, to that.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:02.700
And some of them can be excitory

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:04.620
and some of them can be inhibitory, right?

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.960
When we give people vaccines,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.240
there's one edge that's inhibitory

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:10.620
from your getting the illness,

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.810
but unfortunately another edge,

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:16.230
if it's say a killed live vaccine,

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:19.260
some number of people actually
get the illness, right?

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.560
So there's some chance you'll
be worse off if you take it,

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.050
there's some chance you'll
be better off if you take it.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.590
It really depends a lot on
the weights of those nodes

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.743
to figure out whether you
should take the vaccine, right?

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.237
But then once you see that, you say,

00:14:34.237 --> 00:14:39.060
"Well, for very, very, very
special values of these weights,

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:41.550
I can actually cancel out

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:45.540
an excitory and inhibitory effect."

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.550
So it's zero net effect, right?

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.320
I'm giving people all these vaccines

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:51.810
and I see no difference at
all in their sickness rates.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:52.807
And one thing you say is,

00:14:52.807 --> 00:14:54.990
"Look, vaccine's not doing anything,"

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:57.270
but it could be, "No,
no, no, it's actually...

00:14:57.270 --> 00:15:01.380
It's actually creating
more sickness in this group

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:03.990
and preventing sickness in that group."

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.540
And in fact, different
people would've gotten sick,

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.020
but the statistics would be the same

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:13.050
because these effects just
cancel each other out.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:15.660
Now, one of the things that
happens in this literature

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:17.233
is then people say, "Well..."

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:21.300
All right, this is what, okay.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.960
So I already said that you
need to constrain these graphs.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:27.480
One thing you do is by temporal order.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:31.080
That means you don't allow
retrocausation or causal cycles.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.600
Now, here's where I am.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:37.620
So there's the vaccine example.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:38.940
I've already said this stuff.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:41.430
You can set weights

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:44.550
so that the net effect
will be statistically zero

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.520
even though it's actually
having causal effects.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.267
And so what people do is they say,

00:15:49.267 --> 00:15:54.267
"Look, we've got all of these graphs.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.820
We're trying to cut down the number

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:00.210
of possible causal structures
we're considering."

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.910
Just don't take them seriously

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:05.640
if they're fine-tuned in that way, right?

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.210
So there's this principle
called faithfulness

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.320
or no fine-tuning

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.390
that basically says if
you've set up a graph

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:14.520
and you've put these weights on it

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:17.790
such that a manipulation you can do here

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:21.510
has causal connection to
something observable here

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:23.580
but you don't see any statistical changes

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.830
because of everything's canceling out,

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:26.850
just ignore that.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:27.870
That's fine-tuning.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:31.050
That's possible, but very implausible.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:32.310
Don't believe it.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:33.180
It's like fine...

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:35.640
I mean it's the same fine-tuning people...

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.320
We've talked about in cosmology.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.270
How fine-tuned is the initial state.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.040
How bad is fine-tuning?

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.753
That's all perfectly fine.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:48.480
And what you'd like to do,

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.060
and this is what Glymour was
doing and Pearl was doing,

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:54.840
was just say, "Okay, what
good principles do I have

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:57.510
that will take me from the observed data

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:00.360
to plausible, underlying causal structures

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:02.550
constrained in part by the data,

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:05.850
constrained by other theoretical
considerations sometimes?"

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:07.080
But you see the problem,

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:09.693
you just have this over
generation of possibilities,

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:12.570
and again, the more latent nodes.

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:15.570
And in a lot of cases, most
of these nodes are latent.

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:18.390
I mean this was about
like with the monkeys,

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:19.710
what data can we get, right?

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:24.270
I've got all of this
incredible causal structure,

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.220
but I can only get my hands on the poop.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.170
Okay, well, what can
I do with that, right?

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:33.170
So anyway, so that was a
piece of methodological advice

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.560
that comes in a reasonable way

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:41.460
out of the linear causal graph program

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:45.483
that says if you've got a
theory that's fine-tuned bad,

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:47.910
but then you say, "Well,

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:51.630
what's the indication that
the theory is fine-tuned

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:52.650
and now what they use?"

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.900
Notice if I fine-tune the theory

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:57.900
so that changing this variable here

00:17:57.900 --> 00:17:59.970
has no statistical difference there,

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.100
I also get a no-signaling theory, right?

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.950
I can't send a message from here to here

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.470
by twiddling this knob
'cause you've set things up.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:08.910
So no matter how you turn over the knobs,

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:11.100
the observable statistics anyway

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:12.720
will be the same at the other end.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.000
So then they say, "Oh, so
here's the piece of advice.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.010
If there's no signaling
between this and this,

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.860
there should be no causal
connections, right?"

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:22.320
Reject a theory that
has causal connections

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:23.640
between two nodes.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:25.440
If you can't see,

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.900
if you can manipulate one
and you can see the other,

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:29.010
and these manipulations

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.020
don't make any statistical difference.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:33.690
All right, so that's
the methodological moral

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:35.973
that comes out of that tradition.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.620
You say, "Yeah, of course
logically it's possible

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:42.360
that you've got a causal
connection there."

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:43.530
Logically, it's possible.

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:45.000
But if you can't signal,

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.850
your best advice is to throw
that graph away, right?

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.160
Throw that theory away,
say it's bad, right?

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:52.083
Fine-tune, bad, bad, go away.

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:58.200
This was underlying for
people who know this stuff,

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:02.340
this kind of consideration
was underlying Glymour's work

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:03.660
on the tetra equations,

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:06.840
which is also Pearl
learned a lot from them.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:09.330
Okay, there's this history
there I don't wanna go into.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:11.910
Now, the problem that happened

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.370
is that this piece of
methodological advice

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:19.057
has been wildly oversimplified
to this idea that,

00:19:19.057 --> 00:19:23.880
"Gee, if you can't signal
between here and here,

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.370
then you should never accept a theory

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:29.430
that has a causal connection
between here and here, right?"

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.263
You're violating this principle.

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:34.590
And so I've put it down
at the bottom here.

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:38.160
If there's a causal influence
of the input on the output,

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.170
then you should be able to signal, right?

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:43.320
You should be able to
cause an observable change

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:46.323
in the statistics of the
output by varying the input.

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:51.397
The problem is, so that basically says,

00:19:51.397 --> 00:19:52.980
"If you've got no signaling,

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.890
then that implies no causal connection.

00:19:55.890 --> 00:20:00.030
And if somebody has a theory
that has a causal connection,

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:02.220
throw it away, right?"

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:03.900
You haven't empirically refuted it,

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.493
but it's methodologically bad.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:09.990
Now, what we've already seen
is that when we just looked,

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:11.130
forget about...

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:13.410
Go back to the neuron diagrams.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.720
You can't really
fine-tune a neuron diagram

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:17.940
'cause it doesn't have these
weights in it to fine-tune.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:21.480
It's all just this binary response stuff.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:24.240
And what we saw in the
case of Billy and Susie

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:25.860
is you can have some of these diagrams

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:28.080
where there are causal connections,

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:29.790
but you can't use them to signal

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:34.790
because of the way the inhibitory
nodes have been put in.

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:36.053
Right?

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:40.230
And since...

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:44.220
So here, since there are
no edge weights at all,

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:47.160
the word fine-tuning can't
be the right word here.

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:48.840
I mean, the picture of fine-tuning

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:51.840
really is you've got a
radio with all these knobs

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.390
and you're very carefully
setting a whole bunch of knobs

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:55.680
to get a particular result.

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.170
You're not doing that here, you
can't do it in a neuron doc,

00:20:58.170 --> 00:20:59.523
you don't have those knobs.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:05.670
And so in such a case

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:08.880
where you've got two
different causal structures,

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:10.650
nothing fine-tuned,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:13.290
they have the same empirical consequences.

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:16.380
There just may be nothing
to favor one over the other.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:17.520
And you should just admit that.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:21.510
You might say, "Look, one or
the other disease is right,

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.690
we're just epistemically
blind to it, right?

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.850
We can't get at which one is right

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:29.190
because we have no direct access

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:30.747
to the underlying structure."

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.140
So, to get us now,

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:40.260
I'm going to get us to
the result here, I hope.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:42.810
So, if there is signaling, right?

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:44.700
If I have a variable here

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:48.000
and I can freely set it however I like,

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.580
and on the basis of doing that some,

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:54.030
there's the observable statistics
of something else changed,

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:57.060
then I've got a good Shannon
transmitter right now,

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:58.920
I can apply Shannon information theory.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:01.770
I can say, I want to tell you,
"Are we gonna go to lunch?"

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.690
I manipulate this, you look at the data,

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.510
you say, "Oh, you did that. Okay."

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:10.170
So it's certainly reasonable
demand that if you can signal,

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:14.103
you better have some causal
connections there, right?

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.880
The problem was, right,

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:22.880
and you regard one of these
variables as a free variable

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:27.180
if anybody hasn't read John Bell's paper

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:29.460
on free variables in local causality,

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.250
which is just about this and
he hits all the right notes,

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:33.693
I recommend you read that.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:37.620
I think that's a perfectly
reasonable advice, right?

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:39.540
If you can send signals,

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.160
then yeah, there better be a causal path

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:43.743
running from the
transmitter to the receiver.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:47.670
Now, I should mention here,

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:49.290
when you say there's a causal path,

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:51.180
it's kind of an abstract thing.

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:54.150
It doesn't mean there
has to be a particle,

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:56.664
or energy, or anything like this.

00:22:56.664 --> 00:22:59.100
Standard example is Sherlock Holmes,

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.620
the case of the dog in the night, right,

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:03.240
where Sherlock Holmes...

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:08.240
There's a signal that goes
from the happenings in the barn

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:11.580
out to the house where Sherlock Holmes is

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:12.960
or where the people are.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.440
Why? 'Cause the dog didn't bark.

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:18.030
So there was no energy,

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:21.090
there was no particles,
but there was a signal.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:23.370
Why? Because of the
counterfactual structure.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:25.170
If the murderer had been a stranger,

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:27.870
the dog would've barked,
they would've heard it.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.810
They didn't hear it,
ergo the dog didn't bark,

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:33.240
ergo the murderer was a familiar, okay?

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:35.100
That's a perfectly good Shannon signal,

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.680
even though there's not a physical process

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:39.280
that takes it from here to here.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:42.780
Now, the main thing I want to say.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:45.960
You should not turn that
good piece of advice

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:48.937
around into a bad piece
of advice that says,

00:23:48.937 --> 00:23:50.040
"If you can't signal,

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:52.017
there can't be any causal connections."

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:54.450
Now, you might...

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:56.529
Right, because although signaling

00:23:56.529 --> 00:23:59.675
does indicate at least a
possible causal process,

00:23:59.675 --> 00:24:01.230
if you will,

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:02.430
inability to signal

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:04.950
doesn't indicate lack
of causal connection.

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:08.280
Like, the example I gave
you, I delay Billy's throw.

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:11.040
That really does have an
effect on the causal structure.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.080
It still doesn't allow you to signal

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:15.003
because of the way things are set up.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:20.310
So it's the gap between these,

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:23.580
the gap between causation and signaling

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:24.570
is one of the things

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.940
that sends right at the
center of Bell's theorem

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:28.320
and Bell's inequality, you might say.

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:30.750
But if I can't send a signal,

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:31.583
why should I think

00:24:31.583 --> 00:24:33.360
there's a causal connection at all, right?

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:35.250
What's my grounds?

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:38.070
'Cause that's a good empirical grounds.

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:40.110
And what Bell proved,

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:43.500
and this is where I would give
you the proof if I had time,

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:45.720
that there are certain kinds of statistics

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:50.520
that cannot be recovered from
any locally causal theory.

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:55.520
That is where if you separate
two labs far enough apart,

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.070
nothing that's done in this lab
can have any effect on that.

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:01.140
Nothing that's done or happens in that lab

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:02.167
can have any effect on this.

00:25:02.167 --> 00:25:05.610
So that's a locally causal theory.

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:07.860
Everybody thought the
world was locally causal.

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:10.500
Einstein sure thought the
world was locally causal.

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:12.450
The thing he was always
upset about quantum mechanics

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:13.920
was that it wasn't local

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.750
and he always complained about telepathy

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:17.913
and spooky action at a distance.

00:25:19.503 --> 00:25:24.503
What Bell figured out is that
in any locally causal theory,

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:27.240
there are certain restrictions

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:29.850
on the sorts of statistical correlations

00:25:29.850 --> 00:25:33.000
between what happens in these labs, right?

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.070
So I'll just kind of...

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:36.750
What's gonna happen

00:25:36.750 --> 00:25:38.790
is we're gonna create a pair of particles

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:41.160
or sometimes a triple of
particles here at the center.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:43.050
That's the preparation
of the entangled state.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:45.660
That's red 'cause I can
control that, right?

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:48.390
I know what particles I'm creating.

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:51.870
I send a particle out to
Alice's lab on one side,

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:55.740
the other particle out to
Bob's lab on the other side.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:57.840
Alice and Bob each have a choice

00:25:57.840 --> 00:25:59.910
of how to set their
experimental apparatus.

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:01.860
So those are red circles

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:04.200
and those are their free
manipulable choices.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.360
They can make them however they like.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:09.450
And David has done a lot of work

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:11.343
about making them in funny ways.

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:16.800
Both of those clearly
are causally relevant

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.950
to what happens in the experiment, right?

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:21.570
And the experiment gives rise then

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.043
to some observable output.

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:26.190
And the statistics we have

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:29.040
are about the statistics
of the correlations

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.730
between we know Alice's
choice, we know her output,

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:35.130
we know Bob's choice, we know his output.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:37.380
We know what we prepared,
that's always the same,

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:38.760
that doesn't vary in these cases,

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:40.060
so that's straightforward.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:44.370
And if it was a causally local theory,

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:47.820
because Alice is way over here
and Bob is way over there,

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.340
you're not allowed to put an arrow

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:52.740
going from anywhere on Alice's side

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:54.840
to anywhere over on Bob's side, right?

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:57.090
That's what you're just
prevented from doing.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.270
And what Bell showed is that
if you don't have those,

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:02.760
there are constraints on the statistics.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.730
You can see when you compare
what happens in this lab

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:07.130
to what happens in that lab.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:13.140
Now, what happens is quantum theory

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.990
predicts violations of those constraints

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:17.460
and then what happens even better

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:19.920
is the actual world
violates the constraints.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.770
That's what the Nobel Prize
in Physics two years ago

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:23.603
was about,

00:27:23.603 --> 00:27:25.230
was experimental
verification of violations

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:26.283
of Bell's inequality.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:28.860
So now, you get a clear thing.

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:31.190
I have to put arrows, right?

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.150
So there has to be some arrows

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.830
going one way or the other, right?

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:37.650
This shows the world isn't locally causal.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:39.300
So if I want to causal diagram,

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:42.300
I've gotta have these distant causation.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:43.133
What can I do?

00:27:43.133 --> 00:27:47.190
Well, this is now should
remind you of Billy and Susie,

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:48.240
I hope.

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.530
I can put it that way.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:53.100
I can say, "Oh the thing
is set up so that in fact,

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.620
some of what Alice does
or what happens over there

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:57.210
has an effect on Bob."

00:27:57.210 --> 00:28:00.840
Or I can run the causal
thing the other way.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.660
Of course, if you do this
in a kind of symmetric way,

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:06.903
it won't surprise you that
like the Billy and Susie case,

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:09.660
empirically, it won't make any difference

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:11.340
which way the arrows go, right?

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:13.140
The statistics are gonna be the same.

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:16.710
So you can't tell from
the surface statistics.

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:18.780
You can tell there is an arrow,

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:22.230
but you can't tell
which way it runs, okay?

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:25.170
So that becomes empirically
hidden from you.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:28.440
Now, what determines which graph...

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:29.760
Are you-
- [Moderator] Almost done.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:31.320
- Trying to, okay, I'm almost done.

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:33.570
So I wanna say it's gotta be...

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:36.333
It's gotta be one of these two graphs.

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.080
What determines which graph it is?

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:41.580
In the case of Billy and Susie,

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:43.860
the question was the
timing of the rocks, right?

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:45.390
Was it this one or that one?

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:48.660
Here, it's essentially the
timing of these experiments

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:49.893
that are done distantly.

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:55.890
The obvious way to take
care of this is now,

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:59.070
and now I have to say, I'm sorry Albert,

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:00.480
relativity is wrong.

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:02.700
You put in what's called
a preferred foliation.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:06.120
You put in essentially a
kind of global time function

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:09.300
so that you can say who went first.

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:10.770
You can't do this in relativity.

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:12.030
So you're giving up on relativity,

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:14.240
you're giving up for good reasons.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:16.950
If Alice went first, then it's this graph.

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:19.560
If Bob went first, it's that graph.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:21.300
Now is that fine-tuning, right?

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:23.970
Now, you can easily see,
you can set this up.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:25.980
So you can't tell from
the surface statistics

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:26.813
who went first.

00:29:26.813 --> 00:29:28.683
You can't use this to send signals.

00:29:31.554 --> 00:29:32.850
All right, I'll just...

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:36.600
So they're detailed examples
in pilot wave theory,

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:39.210
you exactly understand how this goes.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:40.830
I'm just gonna skip over this

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:42.730
'cause obviously I'm running too late.

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:46.470
And in the collapse theories,
it goes a different way,

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:47.940
but I can't back that up.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:50.760
So let me just say, in
both of these cases,

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.670
in the pilot wave theory,
in the collapse theories,

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:55.980
you have no signaling theories.

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:57.060
Bell started these.

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:58.020
He not only proved this,

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:00.570
he proved what's sometimes
called the Bell telephone theorem

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:02.940
that says, "Even though there
has to be this connection,

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:04.920
you can't use it to signal."

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:05.760
And that just follows

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:08.823
from certain understanding
of the dynamics.

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:11.850
And I'm just going through

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:14.400
why you get also no
signaling and collapse.

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:15.990
All I want to say at the end of this

00:30:15.990 --> 00:30:17.940
is that if you look at
any of these theories,

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:21.420
they are not, in any
intuitive sense, fine-tuned.

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:23.790
There's no little parameters
that have been set.

00:30:23.790 --> 00:30:26.433
There's no careful adjusting of any knobs.

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:29.730
It's just what determines what cause

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:32.310
what is the actual order in time,

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:35.490
and relative to this foliation
the actual order in time.

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:38.340
That's a fact, it's a physical fact.

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:41.253
We may not be able to get
our hands on it, okay?

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:44.370
I would even say that in these cases,

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.340
it's in a way Billy and
Susie are slightly fine-tuned

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:48.960
'cause they have to throw their rocks

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:50.280
pretty well-coordinated.

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:53.070
But because this just
matters on who goes first,

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:53.903
it doesn't matter.

00:30:53.903 --> 00:30:54.927
There's no fine-tuning like that.

00:30:54.927 --> 00:30:57.600
You got these two events,
one's earlier than the other.

00:30:57.600 --> 00:30:58.700
So that's...

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:03.900
Just saying that that piece
of methodological advice,

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:08.160
which is drawn out of the
linear causal graph tradition

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:12.780
and then thrown over
inconsiderately, I would say,

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:14.220
over to this example.

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:15.577
And people have used it to say,

00:31:15.577 --> 00:31:17.857
"Oh, pilot wave theory, that's no good."

00:31:17.857 --> 00:31:19.537
"Why not?" "It's fine-tuned."

00:31:19.537 --> 00:31:20.767
"What do you mean it's fine-tuned?"

00:31:20.767 --> 00:31:25.500
"Oh, go read the linear
causal graph literature."

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.140
That's all that's happening
here and it's bad.

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:29.580
It's really harming.

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:32.880
It's harming reasonable
discussion of these cases.

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:37.830
So I thought since I was with
people who do this stuff,

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:41.820
I would make a plea to stop doing that.

00:31:41.820 --> 00:31:43.653
Okay, I think that's the end, right?

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:48.360
Yeah, so in sum, good, so the
methodological rule to avoid,

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:50.580
or even forbid fine-tune causal models

00:31:50.580 --> 00:31:52.410
where the criterion of fine-tuning

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:54.630
is no signaling is
completely inappropriate

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:57.030
in the context of these tests.

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:59.133
If the inequality is violated,

00:32:00.030 --> 00:32:01.230
the Bell's inequality,

00:32:01.230 --> 00:32:03.750
that alone implies some
non-local causation,

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:06.660
no theory that's Bell-local
can make these predictions.

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:08.280
It doesn't mean you can tell empirically

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:09.840
which direction the causation goes,

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:12.450
but the inability to do that
does not imply fine-tuning

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:14.700
in any substantial sense of the term.

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:16.547
So that's that.
- Thank you so much

00:32:16.547 --> 00:32:20.397
(audience applauding)

00:32:20.397 --> 00:32:23.553
We have time for one
quick question, Naftali?

00:32:25.517 --> 00:32:27.033
- [Naftali] Yeah, thanks for your talk.

00:32:27.033 --> 00:32:28.650
Yeah, so I totally agree
with what you've said

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:30.690
about fine-tuning.

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:34.080
And I just want to get
clear on, so I mean I...

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:35.130
Someone might see this talk

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:38.340
and think the lesson is that
you shouldn't use causal models

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:39.420
to think about these scenarios.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:40.380
Well, at least what I...

00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:41.213
Yeah, what I would suggest

00:32:41.213 --> 00:32:42.960
is just you should actually
know what you're talking about

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:45.150
because as you pointed out,

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:47.160
yeah, faithfulness does
not equal fine-tuning.

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:48.573
So.
- Oh, right.

00:32:50.927 --> 00:32:54.030
- Great, let's thank all the
speakers from this morning

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:56.315
and head out to lunch upstairs.

00:32:56.315 --> 00:32:59.565
(audience applauding)

